WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.490
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book four.

2
00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:09.350
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:16.110
Unit three: Marketing Theory. Today you will explain marketing effects with result clauses (turning X into Y).

4
00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:19.365
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:23.962
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.817 --> 00:00:26.131
Target audience.

7
00:00:27.117 --> 00:00:31.136
Target audience means the specific group a message is designed to reach.

8
00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:34.980
The target audience is first-time smartphone users.

9
00:00:35.583 --> 00:00:38.851
Your turn. Say: Target audience.

10
00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:43.189
Target audience.

11
00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:44.963
Positioning.

12
00:00:45.950 --> 00:00:49.865
Positioning means shaping how a brand is perceived relative to rivals.

13
00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:54.272
Its positioning as 'affordable luxury' attracted young professionals.

14
00:00:54.883 --> 00:00:57.338
Your turn. Say: Positioning.

15
00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:01.039
Positioning.

16
00:01:01.783 --> 00:01:02.982
Brand equity.

17
00:01:03.983 --> 00:01:09.141
Brand equity means the commercial value that comes from customers' perception of a brand.

18
00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:12.481
Decades of quality built enormous brand equity.

19
00:01:13.083 --> 00:01:15.596
Your turn. Say: Brand equity.

20
00:01:18.433 --> 00:01:19.725
Brand equity.

21
00:01:20.483 --> 00:01:21.949
Consumer insight.

22
00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:26.912
Consumer insight means a deep understanding of what motivates customers.

23
00:01:27.517 --> 00:01:31.269
The key consumer insight was that parents feared unsafe food.

24
00:01:31.883 --> 00:01:35.128
Your turn. Say: Consumer insight.

25
00:01:37.967 --> 00:01:39.479
Consumer insight.

26
00:01:40.217 --> 00:01:41.833
Market segmentation.

27
00:01:42.817 --> 00:01:47.045
Market segmentation means dividing a market into groups with similar needs.

28
00:01:47.650 --> 00:01:51.182
Market segmentation revealed three distinct groups of buyers.

29
00:01:51.783 --> 00:01:54.749
Your turn. Say: Market segmentation.

30
00:01:57.600 --> 00:01:59.274
Market segmentation.

31
00:02:00.017 --> 00:02:01.006
Campaign.

32
00:02:02.017 --> 00:02:05.096
Campaign means a planned series of marketing activities.

33
00:02:05.683 --> 00:02:08.855
The Khmer New Year campaign went viral on TikTok.

34
00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:11.731
Your turn. Say: Campaign.

35
00:02:14.567 --> 00:02:15.846
Campaign.

36
00:02:16.583 --> 00:02:17.584
Conversion.

37
00:02:18.583 --> 00:02:22.951
Conversion means the point at which a prospect takes a desired action, such as buying.

38
00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:26.722
Free delivery raised the conversion rate by 20%.

39
00:02:27.317 --> 00:02:29.830
Your turn. Say: Conversion.

40
00:02:32.667 --> 00:02:33.610
Conversion.

41
00:02:34.350 --> 00:02:35.316
Loyalty.

42
00:02:36.317 --> 00:02:40.069
Loyalty means customers' tendency to keep choosing the same brand.

43
00:02:40.683 --> 00:02:43.275
Loyalty is cheaper to keep than to rebuild.

44
00:02:43.883 --> 00:02:47.012
Your turn. Say: Loyalty.

45
00:02:49.867 --> 00:02:51.088
Loyalty.

46
00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:55.423
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:56.283 --> 00:03:03.220
Visal, a local milk-tea brand spent almost nothing on advertising, yet its queues are endless. What's the secret?

48
00:03:04.317 --> 00:03:14.341
It started with a consumer insight: students wanted somewhere to be seen. The shops are designed for photos, turning every customer into an advertiser.

49
00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:17.995
So social media did the marketing for them?

50
00:03:19.083 --> 00:03:27.030
Partly. Clever positioning turned a cheap drink into an affordable status symbol, which made people willing to queue.

51
00:03:28.117 --> 00:03:30.314
Can that kind of excitement last?

52
00:03:31.417 --> 00:03:40.989
Only if they keep converting interest into loyalty. Hype fades quickly, so they need a reward programme and consistent quality.

53
00:03:42.133 --> 00:03:43.773
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:44.617 --> 00:03:48.474
Grammar focus: Result clauses (turning X into Y).

55
00:03:48.774 --> 00:03:50.135
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:53.813
A great campaign can turn interest into loyalty.

57
00:03:54.717 --> 00:03:58.167
Clever positioning turned a cheap drink into a status symbol.

58
00:03:59.083 --> 00:04:03.730
The shops are designed for photos, turning every customer into an advertiser.

59
00:04:04.667 --> 00:04:13.948
A participle clause (', turning X into Y') adds the result of the whole main clause. Keep the subject the same: the design turns customers into advertisers.

60
00:04:14.683 --> 00:04:18.253
Be careful! Don't say: The ad turned viewers to buyers.

61
00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:23.070
Say: The ad turned viewers into buyers.

62
00:04:23.370 --> 00:04:24.755
Turn X into Y.

63
00:04:25.567 --> 00:04:28.370
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:29.167 --> 00:04:30.911
From Attention to Loyalty.

65
00:04:31.211 --> 00:04:38.795
Marketing theory traditionally describes a funnel in which awareness is gradually transformed into interest, desire and finally action.

66
00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:45.921
Effective marketers begin with segmentation, since a message aimed at everyone usually persuades no one.

67
00:04:46.933 --> 00:04:52.834
A powerful consumer insight can turn an ordinary product into something people feel emotionally connected to.

68
00:04:53.833 --> 00:05:00.442
Digital platforms have reshaped the funnel, turning satisfied customers into influential reviewers almost overnight.

69
00:05:01.433 --> 00:05:08.228
Nevertheless, the ultimate test of any campaign is whether it builds lasting brand equity rather than a brief spike in sales.

70
00:05:09.333 --> 00:05:12.952
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:13.817 --> 00:05:17.128
The app turned casual users blank paying subscribers.

72
00:05:17.428 --> 00:05:20.420
A, to. B, into. C, for.

73
00:05:23.967 --> 00:05:25.897
The answer is B: into.

74
00:05:27.050 --> 00:05:30.245
Dividing a market into groups with similar needs is blank.

75
00:05:30.545 --> 00:05:35.151
A, conversion. B, loyalty. C, market segmentation.

76
00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:41.547
The answer is C: market segmentation.

77
00:05:42.683 --> 00:05:46.494
The commercial value of customers' perception of a brand is blank.

78
00:05:46.794 --> 00:05:51.655
A, brand equity. B, a campaign. C, a target audience.

79
00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:57.385
The answer is A: brand equity.

80
00:05:58.550 --> 00:06:00.236
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:06:00.536 --> 00:06:11.167
A, The video went viral, turning the shop into a landmark.. B, The video went viral, turned the shop into a landmark.. C, The video went viral, turning the shop to landmark..

82
00:06:14.733 --> 00:06:19.252
The answer is A: The video went viral, turning the shop into a landmark..

83
00:06:20.417 --> 00:06:23.879
According to the reading, the ultimate test of a campaign is whether it…

84
00:06:24.179 --> 00:06:29.678
A, wins awards. B, builds lasting brand equity. C, costs very little.

85
00:06:33.233 --> 00:06:36.278
The answer is B: builds lasting brand equity.

86
00:06:37.467 --> 00:06:40.932
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:41.783 --> 00:06:45.153
What is one way a company can build genuine brand loyalty?

88
00:07:01.950 --> 00:07:03.485
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:07:03.785 --> 00:07:39.673
One effective way to build genuine brand loyalty is to deliver a consistent experience that matches the brand's promise every single time. Discounts can turn first-time buyers into repeat customers, but they rarely create emotional attachment. In contrast, a company that listens to feedback and responds quickly makes customers feel respected. A café that remembers regulars' orders can turn visitors into loyal ambassadors. In my view, trust built over time is far more powerful than any promotion.

90
00:07:39.973 --> 00:07:41.752
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:42.650 --> 00:07:47.497
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and result clauses (turning x into y).

92
00:07:47.797 --> 00:07:52.388
Today's value is customer focus. Listen carefully to the people you serve.

93
00:07:52.688 --> 00:07:55.038
See you in Unit four. Goodbye!

94
00:07:55.338 --> 00:07:58.142
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

